module

public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.DifferentialGraded.Module.CompactCountableCohomology
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.DifferentialGraded.Module.DerivedHomCoproducts

/-!
# A module with countable cohomology is a derived colimit of compact objects

Let `(A, d)` be a differential graded algebra over a commutative ring `R` whose cohomology
`H^k(A)` is countable in every degree, and let `M` be an object of the derived category
`D(A, d)`. This file proves the converse implication of [Stacks, Tag 0CRM]: if `H^i(M)` is
countable in every degree then `M` is a derived colimit `hocolim Eₙ` of a system of *compact*
objects. Together with the implication proved in
`StacksFrontier/Algebra/DifferentialGraded/Module/CompactCountableCohomology.lean` this gives the
equivalence of the tag.

## Main results

* `DifferentialGradedModule.exists_isHomotopyColimit_isCompactObject_of_countable_cohomology`:
  **Stacks 0CRM**, the implication (2) ⇒ (1).
* `DifferentialGradedModule.exists_isHomotopyColimit_isCompactObject_iff_countable_cohomology`:
  **Stacks 0CRM**, the equivalence of (1) and (2).
* `DifferentialGradedModule.exists_isCompactObject_comp_eq_forall_comp_eq_zero`: finitely many
  cells of a compact approximation of `M` which die in `M` can be killed simultaneously by one
  cell attachment, the attached object staying compact.

## Implementation notes

*The route taken, and why it is not the source's.* The source builds a countable differential
graded *subalgebra* `A' ⊆ A` and transports the problem to `D(A', d)`. This repository has no
notion of a graded or differential graded subalgebra of a `DifferentialGradedAlgebra` — such a
thing would require transporting a `DirectSum.Decomposition` onto a subalgebra, which Mathlib
does not provide — so that route is unavailable here. (The source's step "the subalgebra generated
by a countable set `S` is countable" is also imprecise as written: it needs `R` countable, which
Tag 0CRM does not hypothesise.) What is used instead is the cell-tower machinery of
[Stacks, Tag 09SN], which is available for any pre-triangulated category generated by compact
objects, applied to the single compact generator `A` of `D(A, d)`
(`DifferentialGradedModule.isWeakTriangulatedGenerator_q_regularModule`).

*Why Tag 09SN alone is not enough, and what a diagonal refinement adds.* The tower constructed by
`CategoryTheory.Pretriangulated.exists_isHomotopyColimit_of_compactlyGenerated` attaches *all*
cells at stage `0` and *all* dead cells at each later stage, so its terms are coproducts over
index types which are in general infinite and its terms are therefore not compact. The
construction below attaches only finitely many cells per stage, which keeps the terms compact
(`CategoryTheory.isCompactObject_sigma`), and arranges by a diagonal enumeration that every cell
is nevertheless dealt with at some stage. This is what
`CategoryTheory.Pretriangulated.isHomotopyColimit_of_forall_fac_of_forall_eventually_eq_zero`
is for: it asks only that a dead cell at stage `n` die at *some* later stage, not at stage
`n + 1`.

The bookkeeping is as follows. A *cell* of an object `X` is a pair `(m, ρ)` with
`ρ : A[m] ⟶ X`; cells of `X` are the same thing as cohomology classes of `X`
(`DifferentialGradedModule.cohomologyEquivHomRegularShift`), so the cells of `X` form a countable
set as soon as `H^*(X)` is countable. Each stage of the tower carries, as part of its data, a
surjection `eₙ : ℕ → {cells of Eₙ}`; the step from `Eₙ` to `Eₙ₊₁` attaches the `n`-th cell of `M`
and kills every dead cell among `eₙ 0, …, eₙ (4ⁿ - 1)`, and the new enumeration is chosen so that
`eₙ₊₁ (2k)` is `eₙ k` pushed forward. A cell sitting at index `k` at stage `n` therefore sits at
index `2ˢ k` at stage `n + s`, and `2ˢ k < 4^(n + s)` holds for `s = k + 1`; so it is inside the
window at some later stage and is killed there. The window `4ⁿ` and the doubling `2k` are
calibrated against each other by
`DifferentialGradedModule.exists_two_pow_mul_lt_four_pow` — any window growing faster than the
doubling would do.

*Where countability of `H^*(A)` is used.* Exactly once, and only for the stages: a stage `Eₙ` is
compact, so `H^*(Eₙ)` is countable by
`DifferentialGradedModule.countable_cohomology_of_isCompactObject`, which is the one result that
consumes the hypothesis `hA`; that countability is what provides the surjection
`ℕ → {cells of Eₙ}` carried by the next stage
(`DifferentialGradedModule.exists_surjective_cell`). Countability of `H^*(M)` itself — hypothesis
(2) — is used only to enumerate the cells of `M`, which is what makes the attached cells exhaust
`H^*(M)`.

*Universes.* Compactness is read here in the repository's universe-restricted sense
`IsCompactObject.{u}`, which tests only families indexed by `Type u`, whereas the source
quantifies over arbitrary index sets. Source-compactness implies `IsCompactObject.{u}` and the
converse is proved nowhere, so a *conclusion* of compactness in this spelling is **stronger**
than the source's: the statement proved below is stronger than Tag 0CRM's (2) ⇒ (1), not weaker.
This is the same reading as in
`StacksFrontier/Algebra/DifferentialGraded/Module/CompactCountableCohomology.lean`, whose
implementation notes record the same point for the cell-attachment results; the objects produced
here are built from `A` by shifts, finite direct sums and cones, so the restriction is no loss in
practice. Note also that the morphisms of `derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A` live in `Type (u + 1)`,
while its direct sums and its compactness are indexed by `Type u`; this is why the gluing
criterion of Tag 09SN had to be made polymorphic in the universe against which compactness is
tested.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0CRM](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CRM); the cell tower is
  [Tag 09SN](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09SN), the definition of a derived colimit is
  [Tag 090Z](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/090Z).
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits CategoryTheory.Pretriangulated

universe u

namespace DifferentialGradedModule

variable {R : Type u} [CommRing R]

/- As in the file carrying the implication (1) ⇒ (2), the derived colimit and the cohomology
functor are read in the cone-triangle structure on `K(A, d)`, which is given priority here. -/
attribute [local instance 10000] instPretriangulatedHomotopyCategory

variable (A : DifferentialGradedAlgebra.{u, u} R)

/-! ### Cells -/

/-- A **cell** of an object `X` of `D(A, d)`: a degree `m` together with a morphism `A[m] ⟶ X`
out of the corresponding shift of the regular module. Cells of `X` are the same thing as
cohomology classes of `X` (`DifferentialGradedModule.cohomologyEquivHomRegularShift`), and they
are exactly the morphisms against which the generation criterion for `D(A, d)` is tested. -/
private abbrev Cell (X : derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A) :=
  Σ m : ℤ, ((regularObject A)⟦m⟧ ⟶ X)

/-- A cell of `X` pushed forward along a morphism `X ⟶ Y`. -/
private def pushCell {X Y : derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A} (f : X ⟶ Y) (t : Cell A X) : Cell A Y :=
  ⟨t.1, t.2 ≫ f⟩

/-- Pushing forward twice is pushing forward along the composition. -/
private theorem pushCell_pushCell {X Y Z : derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A} (f : X ⟶ Y) (g : Y ⟶ Z)
    (t : Cell A X) : pushCell A g (pushCell A f t) = pushCell A (f ≫ g) t := by
  simp [pushCell]

/-- An object with countable cohomology has countably many cells: a cell of degree `m` is a
cohomology class of degree `-m`, and there are countably many degrees. -/
private theorem countable_cell (X : derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A)
    (hX : ∀ i : ℤ, Countable ((cohomologyFunctor A i).obj X)) : Countable (Cell A X) := by
  have h : ∀ m : ℤ, Countable ((regularObject A)⟦m⟧ ⟶ X) := fun m => by
    have e := cohomologyEquivHomRegularShift A (-m) X
    rw [neg_neg] at e
    exact @Countable.of_equiv _ _ (hX (-m)) e
  exact @instCountableSigma _ _ _ h

/-- The cells of an object with countable cohomology can be enumerated: the set of cells is
countable, and nonempty because the zero morphism is one. -/
private theorem exists_surjective_cell_of_countable (X : derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A)
    (hX : ∀ i : ℤ, Countable ((cohomologyFunctor A i).obj X)) :
    ∃ e : ℕ → Cell A X, Function.Surjective e :=
  have := countable_cell A X hX
  have : Nonempty (Cell A X) := ⟨⟨0, 0⟩⟩
  exists_surjective_nat _

/-- **The cells of a compact object can be enumerated.** This is the only place the hypothesis
that `H^*(A)` is countable is used: a compact object has countable cohomology
(`DifferentialGradedModule.countable_cohomology_of_isCompactObject`), hence countably many
cells. -/
private theorem exists_surjective_cell (hA : ∀ k : ℤ, Countable (A.Cohomology k))
    (X : derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A) (hX : IsCompactObject.{u} X) :
    ∃ e : ℕ → Cell A X, Function.Surjective e :=
  exists_surjective_cell_of_countable A X
    fun i => countable_cohomology_of_isCompactObject A hA X hX i

/-! ### Attaching finitely many cells at once -/

/-- **Finitely many cells dying in `M` can be killed by a single cell attachment.** Let `E` be a
compact object of `D(A, d)` and `u : E ⟶ M` a morphism, and let `ρ j : A[m j] ⟶ E` be a finite
family of morphisms all of which `u` annihilates. Then there is a morphism `f : E ⟶ E'` into a
compact object through which `u` factors and which kills every `ρ j`.

The object `E'` is the cone on the morphism `∐ⱼ A[m j] ⟶ E` assembled from the `ρ j`; it is
compact because a finite direct sum of compact objects is compact
(`CategoryTheory.isCompactObject_sigma`) and the third vertex of a distinguished triangle on
compact objects is compact (Tag 07LS). This is the simultaneous form of
`DifferentialGradedModule.exists_isCompactObject_comp_eq_cohomologyFunctor_map_eq_zero`, which
kills one prescribed cohomology class; attaching finitely many cells at a time, rather than all
of them, is what keeps the stages of the tower below compact. -/
theorem exists_isCompactObject_comp_eq_forall_comp_eq_zero
    {M E : derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A} (hE : IsCompactObject.{u} E) (u : E ⟶ M) {J : Type u}
    [Finite J] (m : J → ℤ) (ρ : ∀ j, (regularObject A)⟦m j⟧ ⟶ E) (hρ : ∀ j, ρ j ≫ u = 0) :
    ∃ (E' : derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A) (f : E ⟶ E') (u' : E' ⟶ M),
      IsCompactObject.{u} E' ∧ f ≫ u' = u ∧ ∀ j, ρ j ≫ f = 0 := by
  have hP : IsCompactObject.{u} (regularObject A) := isCompactObject_q_regularModule A
  have hZ : IsCompactObject.{u} (∐ fun j => (regularObject A)⟦m j⟧) :=
    isCompactObject_sigma _ fun j => isCompactObject_shift hP (m j)
  have hcu : Limits.Sigma.desc ρ ≫ u = 0 := by
    refine Limits.Sigma.hom_ext _ _ fun j => ?_
    rw [← Category.assoc, Limits.Sigma.ι_desc, hρ j, comp_zero]
  obtain ⟨E', f, w, hT⟩ := Pretriangulated.distinguished_cocone_triangle (Limits.Sigma.desc ρ)
  obtain ⟨u', hu'⟩ := Pretriangulated.Triangle.yoneda_exact₂ _ hT u hcu
  refine ⟨E', f, u', isCompactObject_obj₃ _ hT hZ hE, hu'.symm, fun j => ?_⟩
  have h0 : Limits.Sigma.desc ρ ≫ f = 0 := Pretriangulated.comp_distTriang_mor_zero₁₂ _ hT
  rw [← Limits.Sigma.ι_desc ρ j, Category.assoc, h0, comp_zero]

/-! ### The diagonal window -/

/-- **The window of the diagonal enumeration outgrows the doubling of the indices.** A cell at
index `k` of the enumeration of stage `n` sits at index `2ˢ k` of the enumeration of stage
`n + s`, and the window treated at that stage is `4^(n + s)`; this says that the index is inside
the window for some `s`, namely `s = k + 1`. -/
private theorem exists_two_pow_mul_lt_four_pow (n k : ℕ) : ∃ s : ℕ, 2 ^ s * k < 4 ^ (n + s) := by
  refine ⟨k + 1, ?_⟩
  have h1 : k < 2 ^ (k + 1) :=
    lt_of_lt_of_le Nat.lt_two_pow_self (Nat.pow_le_pow_right (by norm_num) (by omega))
  have h2 : 2 ^ (k + 1) * k < 2 ^ (k + 1) * 2 ^ (k + 1) :=
    (Nat.mul_lt_mul_left (Nat.two_pow_pos _)).mpr h1
  have h3 : (2 : ℕ) ^ (k + 1) * 2 ^ (k + 1) = 4 ^ (k + 1) := by
    rw [show (4 : ℕ) = 2 * 2 by norm_num, Nat.mul_pow]
  exact lt_of_lt_of_le (h3 ▸ h2) (Nat.pow_le_pow_right (by norm_num) (by omega))

/-! ### Stages of the tower -/

/-- A **stage** of the approximation of `M` by compact objects: a compact object with a morphism
to `M`, together with an enumeration of its cells. The enumeration is part of the data rather
than produced on demand, because the diagonal argument has to compare the enumerations of
different stages. -/
private structure Stage (M : derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A) where
  /-- the object of the stage -/
  obj : derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A
  /-- the object of the stage is compact -/
  compact : IsCompactObject.{u} obj
  /-- the structure morphism to `M` -/
  hom : obj ⟶ M
  /-- an enumeration of the cells of the stage -/
  enum : ℕ → Cell A obj
  /-- the enumeration is onto -/
  enum_surjective : Function.Surjective enum

/-- One step of the tower out of the stage `s`, the `n`-th step, attaching the cell `t` of `M`:
the next stage, the transition morphism, and the three properties the diagonal argument needs —
the dead cells in the window `4 ^ n` of the current enumeration die, the new enumeration extends
the pushforward of the current one along the even indices, and the prescribed cell `t` of `M` is
reached from the new stage. -/
private structure StepData {M : derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A} (s : Stage A M) (n : ℕ)
    (t : Cell A M) where
  /-- the next stage -/
  next : Stage A M
  /-- the transition morphism -/
  hom : s.obj ⟶ next.obj
  /-- the structure morphisms are compatible with the transition -/
  comm : hom ≫ next.hom = s.hom
  /-- a cell in the window which dies in `M` dies in the next stage -/
  kill : ∀ j : ℕ, j < 4 ^ n → (s.enum j).2 ≫ s.hom = 0 → (s.enum j).2 ≫ hom = 0
  /-- the new enumeration extends the pushforward of the old one along the even indices -/
  enum_two_mul : ∀ k : ℕ, next.enum (2 * k) = pushCell A hom (s.enum k)
  /-- the prescribed cell of `M` is reached from the next stage -/
  hit : ∃ r : (regularObject A)⟦t.1⟧ ⟶ next.obj, r ≫ next.hom = t.2

/-- **A step out of any stage exists.** The next stage is obtained in two moves: first the
prescribed cell of `M` is attached, by forming the direct sum of the stage with `A[t.1]` and
extending the structure morphism by the morphism naming the cell; then the dead cells of the
window — finitely many — are killed at once by
`DifferentialGradedModule.exists_isCompactObject_comp_eq_forall_comp_eq_zero`. Both moves keep
the object compact, so its cells can be enumerated
(`DifferentialGradedModule.exists_surjective_cell`, where the countability of `H^*(A)` enters),
and the new enumeration is interleaved with the pushforward of the old one so that the even
indices carry the latter. -/
private theorem nonempty_stepData (hA : ∀ k : ℤ, Countable (A.Cohomology k))
    {M : derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A} (s : Stage A M) (n : ℕ) (t : Cell A M) :
    Nonempty (StepData A s n t) := by
  have hP : IsCompactObject.{u} (regularObject A) := isCompactObject_q_regularModule A
  -- attach the prescribed cell of `M`
  set E₁ := s.obj ⊞ (regularObject A)⟦t.1⟧
  set u₁ : E₁ ⟶ M := biprod.desc s.hom t.2
  have hc₁ : IsCompactObject.{u} E₁ :=
    isCompactObject_biprod s.compact (isCompactObject_shift hP t.1)
  have hinl : (biprod.inl : s.obj ⟶ E₁) ≫ u₁ = s.hom := biprod.inl_desc _ _
  have hinr : (biprod.inr : (regularObject A)⟦t.1⟧ ⟶ E₁) ≫ u₁ = t.2 := biprod.inr_desc _ _
  -- kill the dead cells of the window
  set J := ULift.{u} {j : Fin (4 ^ n) // (s.enum j.1).2 ≫ s.hom = 0}
  obtain ⟨E₂, f₂, u₂, hc₂, hf₂, hkill⟩ :=
    exists_isCompactObject_comp_eq_forall_comp_eq_zero A hc₁ u₁
      (J := J) (fun j => (s.enum j.down.1.1).1)
      (fun j => (s.enum j.down.1.1).2 ≫ biprod.inl)
      (fun j => by rw [Category.assoc, hinl]; exact j.down.2)
  -- enumerate the cells of the new stage
  obtain ⟨g, hg⟩ := exists_surjective_cell A hA E₂ hc₂
  refine ⟨{
    next :=
      { obj := E₂
        compact := hc₂
        hom := u₂
        enum := fun j => if j % 2 = 0 then pushCell A (biprod.inl ≫ f₂) (s.enum (j / 2))
          else g (j / 2)
        enum_surjective := fun x => ?_ }
    hom := biprod.inl ≫ f₂
    comm := ?_
    kill := ?_
    enum_two_mul := ?_
    hit := ⟨biprod.inr ≫ f₂, ?_⟩ }⟩
  · obtain ⟨k, rfl⟩ := hg x
    exact ⟨2 * k + 1, by simp [Nat.mul_add_div]⟩
  · rw [Category.assoc, hf₂, hinl]
  · intro j hj hdead
    simpa using hkill (ULift.up ⟨⟨j, hj⟩, hdead⟩)
  · intro k
    simp [Nat.mul_div_cancel_left k (by norm_num : 0 < 2)]
  · rw [Category.assoc, hf₂, hinr]

/-! ### The tower -/

/-- The chosen step out of a stage, attaching the `n`-th cell of the enumeration `c` of the cells
of `M`. -/
private noncomputable def stepData (hA : ∀ k : ℤ, Countable (A.Cohomology k))
    {M : derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A} (c : ℕ → Cell A M) (s : Stage A M) (n : ℕ) :
    StepData A s n (c n) :=
  (nonempty_stepData A hA s n (c n)).some

/-- The stages of the tower over `M` starting at `s₀`. -/
private noncomputable def tower (hA : ∀ k : ℤ, Countable (A.Cohomology k))
    {M : derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A} (c : ℕ → Cell A M) (s₀ : Stage A M) :
    ℕ → Stage A M := fun n =>
  Nat.rec s₀ (fun k s => (stepData A hA c s k).next) n

/-- The tower over `M` starting at `s₀`, as a system of objects indexed by `ℕ`. -/
private noncomputable def cellTower (hA : ∀ k : ℤ, Countable (A.Cohomology k))
    {M : derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A} (c : ℕ → Cell A M) (s₀ : Stage A M) :
    ℕ ⥤ derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A :=
  Functor.ofSequence (X := fun n => (tower A hA c s₀ n).obj)
    fun n => (stepData A hA c (tower A hA c s₀ n) n).hom

section Interface

variable (hA : ∀ k : ℤ, Countable (A.Cohomology k)) {M : derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A}

/-- The structure morphism of the `n`-th term of the tower, with its source read as a term of
`cellTower`. All the statements below are phrased against `cellTower` rather than against
`tower`, so that no rewriting has to cross the identification of the two. -/
private noncomputable def towerHom (c : ℕ → Cell A M) (s₀ : Stage A M) (n : ℕ) :
    (cellTower A hA c s₀).obj n ⟶ M :=
  (tower A hA c s₀ n).hom

/-- The enumeration of the cells of the `n`-th term of the tower, read on `cellTower`. -/
private noncomputable def towerEnum (c : ℕ → Cell A M) (s₀ : Stage A M) (n : ℕ) :
    ℕ → Cell A ((cellTower A hA c s₀).obj n) :=
  (tower A hA c s₀ n).enum

/-- Every term of the tower is compact: this is what the whole construction is for. -/
private theorem isCompactObject_cellTower_obj (c : ℕ → Cell A M) (s₀ : Stage A M) (n : ℕ) :
    IsCompactObject.{u} ((cellTower A hA c s₀).obj n) :=
  (tower A hA c s₀ n).compact

/-- The enumeration carried by a term of the tower is onto its cells. -/
private theorem towerEnum_surjective (c : ℕ → Cell A M) (s₀ : Stage A M) (n : ℕ) :
    Function.Surjective (towerEnum A hA c s₀ n) :=
  (tower A hA c s₀ n).enum_surjective

/-- The transition of the tower out of stage `n` is the chosen step morphism. -/
private theorem cellTower_map_succ (c : ℕ → Cell A M) (s₀ : Stage A M) (n : ℕ) :
    (cellTower A hA c s₀).map (homOfLE (Nat.le_succ n))
      = (stepData A hA c (tower A hA c s₀ n) n).hom :=
  Functor.ofSequence_map_homOfLE_succ _ n

/-- The transition of the tower to stage `n + (s + 1)` factors through stage `n + s`. -/
private theorem cellTower_map_add_succ (c : ℕ → Cell A M) (s₀ : Stage A M) (n s : ℕ) :
    (cellTower A hA c s₀).map (homOfLE (Nat.le_add_right n (s + 1)))
      = (cellTower A hA c s₀).map (homOfLE (Nat.le_add_right n s)) ≫
        (cellTower A hA c s₀).map (homOfLE (Nat.le_succ (n + s))) := by
  rw [← Functor.map_comp, homOfLE_comp]

/-- The structure morphisms of the tower are compatible with its transitions. -/
private theorem cellTower_map_succ_comp_towerHom (c : ℕ → Cell A M) (s₀ : Stage A M) (n : ℕ) :
    (cellTower A hA c s₀).map (homOfLE (Nat.le_succ n)) ≫ towerHom A hA c s₀ (n + 1)
      = towerHom A hA c s₀ n := by
  rw [cellTower_map_succ]
  exact (stepData A hA c (tower A hA c s₀ n) n).comm

/-- The even indices of the enumeration at stage `n + 1` carry the pushforward of the enumeration
at stage `n`. -/
private theorem towerEnum_two_mul (c : ℕ → Cell A M) (s₀ : Stage A M) (n k : ℕ) :
    towerEnum A hA c s₀ (n + 1) (2 * k)
      = pushCell A ((cellTower A hA c s₀).map (homOfLE (Nat.le_succ n)))
          (towerEnum A hA c s₀ n k) := by
  rw [cellTower_map_succ]
  exact (stepData A hA c (tower A hA c s₀ n) n).enum_two_mul k

/-- A cell inside the window at stage `n` which dies in `M` dies at stage `n + 1`. -/
private theorem comp_cellTower_map_succ_eq_zero (c : ℕ → Cell A M) (s₀ : Stage A M) (n j : ℕ)
    (hj : j < 4 ^ n) (hdead : (towerEnum A hA c s₀ n j).2 ≫ towerHom A hA c s₀ n = 0) :
    (towerEnum A hA c s₀ n j).2 ≫ (cellTower A hA c s₀).map (homOfLE (Nat.le_succ n)) = 0 := by
  rw [cellTower_map_succ]
  exact (stepData A hA c (tower A hA c s₀ n) n).kill j hj hdead

/-- The `n`-th cell of `M` in the enumeration `c` is reached from stage `n + 1`. -/
private theorem exists_comp_towerHom_succ_eq (c : ℕ → Cell A M) (s₀ : Stage A M) (n : ℕ) :
    ∃ r : (regularObject A)⟦(c n).1⟧ ⟶ (cellTower A hA c s₀).obj (n + 1),
      r ≫ towerHom A hA c s₀ (n + 1) = (c n).2 :=
  (stepData A hA c (tower A hA c s₀ n) n).hit

/-! ### The two hypotheses of the gluing criterion -/

/-- The structure morphisms of the tower are compatible with its transitions, for an arbitrary
later index. -/
private theorem towerHom_eq_map_comp_towerHom (c : ℕ → Cell A M) (s₀ : Stage A M) (n s : ℕ) :
    towerHom A hA c s₀ n
      = (cellTower A hA c s₀).map (homOfLE (Nat.le_add_right n s))
        ≫ towerHom A hA c s₀ (n + s) := by
  induction s with
  | zero => simp
  | succ s ih =>
    show towerHom A hA c s₀ n
      = (cellTower A hA c s₀).map (homOfLE (Nat.le_add_right n (s + 1)))
        ≫ towerHom A hA c s₀ (n + s + 1)
    rw [ih, ← cellTower_map_succ_comp_towerHom A hA c s₀ (n + s), ← Category.assoc,
      ← cellTower_map_add_succ]

/-- **The index of a cell doubles at each stage.** The cell at index `k` of the enumeration of
stage `n` is, pushed forward to stage `n + s`, the cell at index `2ˢ k` there. -/
private theorem towerEnum_two_pow_mul (c : ℕ → Cell A M) (s₀ : Stage A M) (n k s : ℕ) :
    towerEnum A hA c s₀ (n + s) (2 ^ s * k)
      = pushCell A ((cellTower A hA c s₀).map (homOfLE (Nat.le_add_right n s)))
          (towerEnum A hA c s₀ n k) := by
  induction s with
  | zero => simp [pushCell]
  | succ s ih =>
    show towerEnum A hA c s₀ (n + s + 1) (2 ^ (s + 1) * k)
      = pushCell A ((cellTower A hA c s₀).map (homOfLE (Nat.le_add_right n (s + 1))))
          (towerEnum A hA c s₀ n k)
    rw [show 2 ^ (s + 1) * k = 2 * (2 ^ s * k) by ring, towerEnum_two_mul, ih,
      pushCell_pushCell, ← cellTower_map_add_succ]

/-- **Every cell of `M` is reached from some stage of the tower**, since the cells of `M` are
enumerated by `c` and the `n`-th one is attached at the `n`-th step. This is the first hypothesis
of the gluing criterion. -/
private theorem exists_comp_eq_of_surjective (c : ℕ → Cell A M) (s₀ : Stage A M)
    (hc : Function.Surjective c) (t : Cell A M) :
    ∃ (n : ℕ) (r : (regularObject A)⟦t.1⟧ ⟶ (cellTower A hA c s₀).obj n),
      t.2 = r ≫ towerHom A hA c s₀ n := by
  obtain ⟨n, hcn⟩ := hc t
  have hit := exists_comp_towerHom_succ_eq A hA c s₀ n
  rw [hcn] at hit
  obtain ⟨r, hr⟩ := hit
  exact ⟨n + 1, r, hr.symm⟩

/-- **A cell of a stage which dies in `M` dies at some later stage**, which is the second
hypothesis of the gluing criterion. The cell sits at some index `k` of the enumeration of its
stage `n`; pushed forward to stage `n + w` it sits at index `2 ^ w * k`, which for a suitable `w`
is inside the window `4 ^ (n + w)` treated at that stage
(`DifferentialGradedModule.exists_two_pow_mul_lt_four_pow`); and it still dies in `M` there, so
it is killed by the transition to stage `n + w + 1`. -/
private theorem exists_comp_map_eq_zero (c : ℕ → Cell A M) (s₀ : Stage A M) (n : ℕ) (m : ℤ)
    (ρ : (regularObject A)⟦m⟧ ⟶ (cellTower A hA c s₀).obj n)
    (hρ : ρ ≫ towerHom A hA c s₀ n = 0) :
    ∃ s : ℕ, ρ ≫ (cellTower A hA c s₀).map (homOfLE (Nat.le_add_right n s)) = 0 := by
  obtain ⟨k, hk⟩ := towerEnum_surjective A hA c s₀ n ⟨m, ρ⟩
  obtain ⟨w, hw⟩ := exists_two_pow_mul_lt_four_pow n k
  have henum : towerEnum A hA c s₀ (n + w) (2 ^ w * k)
      = ⟨m, ρ ≫ (cellTower A hA c s₀).map (homOfLE (Nat.le_add_right n w))⟩ := by
    rw [towerEnum_two_pow_mul, hk]
    rfl
  have hdead : (towerEnum A hA c s₀ (n + w) (2 ^ w * k)).2 ≫ towerHom A hA c s₀ (n + w) = 0 := by
    rw [henum]
    show (ρ ≫ (cellTower A hA c s₀).map (homOfLE (Nat.le_add_right n w)))
      ≫ towerHom A hA c s₀ (n + w) = 0
    rw [Category.assoc, ← towerHom_eq_map_comp_towerHom, hρ]
  have hk2 := comp_cellTower_map_succ_eq_zero A hA c s₀ (n + w) (2 ^ w * k) hw hdead
  rw [henum] at hk2
  refine ⟨w + 1, ?_⟩
  show ρ ≫ (cellTower A hA c s₀).map (homOfLE (Nat.le_add_right n (w + 1))) = 0
  rw [cellTower_map_add_succ, ← Category.assoc]
  exact hk2

end Interface

/-! ### The regular module as a one-element generating family -/

/-- The image of the regular module in `D(A, d)`, as a family indexed by a one-element type: the
form in which the cell-tower machinery takes its generators. -/
private def regularFamily : PUnit.{u + 1} → derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A := fun _ => regularObject A

private instance isCompactObject_regularFamily (i : PUnit.{u + 1}) :
    IsCompactObject.{u} (regularFamily A i) :=
  isCompactObject_q_regularModule A

/-- **The one-element family generates `D(A, d)`**: an object receiving only zero morphisms from
the shifts of the regular module is zero. This is
`DifferentialGradedModule.isWeakTriangulatedGenerator_q_regularModule` read for the one-element
family, the property of belonging to which is the property of being the regular object. -/
private theorem isZero_of_rightOrthogonal_regularFamily (Y : derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A)
    (hY : ((ObjectProperty.ofObj (regularFamily A)).shiftClosure ℤ).rightOrthogonal Y) :
    IsZero Y := by
  have hgen : ObjectProperty.ofObj (regularFamily A)
      = ObjectProperty.singleton (regularObject A) := by
    ext X
    simp [ObjectProperty.ofObj_iff, regularFamily]
  rw [hgen] at hY
  exact isWeakTriangulatedGenerator_q_regularModule A Y hY

/-! ### The converse implication and the equivalence -/

/-- **Stacks 0CRM, the implication (2) ⇒ (1).** *"Let `(A, d)` be a differential graded algebra
such that `H^i(A)` is countable for every `i`. Let `M` be an object of `D(A, d)`. If `H^i(M)` is
countable for every `i` then `M = hocolim Eₙ` with each `Eₙ` compact."*

The system is the diagonally refined cell tower described in the implementation notes of this
file: at the `n`-th step the `n`-th cell of `M` is attached and the dead cells in the window
`4 ^ n` of the current enumeration are killed, finitely many at a time, so that every term stays
compact; the two hypotheses of the gluing criterion
`CategoryTheory.Pretriangulated.isHomotopyColimit_of_forall_fac_of_forall_eventually_eq_zero`
are `DifferentialGradedModule.exists_comp_eq_of_surjective` and
`DifferentialGradedModule.exists_comp_map_eq_zero`.

Compactness is concluded in the universe-restricted spelling `IsCompactObject.{u}`, which makes
this statement stronger than the source's; see the implementation notes. -/
@[stacks 0CRM "(2) implies (1)"]
theorem exists_isHomotopyColimit_isCompactObject_of_countable_cohomology
    (hA : ∀ k : ℤ, Countable (A.Cohomology k)) (M : derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A)
    (hM : ∀ i : ℤ, Countable ((cohomologyFunctor A i).obj M)) :
    ∃ F : ℕ ⥤ derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A,
      (∀ n : ℕ, IsCompactObject.{u} (F.obj n)) ∧ IsHomotopyColimit F M := by
  obtain ⟨c, hc⟩ := exists_surjective_cell_of_countable A M hM
  obtain ⟨e₀, he₀⟩ :=
    exists_surjective_cell A hA (regularObject A) (isCompactObject_q_regularModule A)
  let s₀ : Stage A M := ⟨regularObject A, isCompactObject_q_regularModule A, 0, e₀, he₀⟩
  refine ⟨cellTower A hA c s₀, isCompactObject_cellTower_obj A hA c s₀, ?_⟩
  refine isHomotopyColimit_of_forall_fac_of_forall_eventually_eq_zero (regularFamily A)
    (isZero_of_rightOrthogonal_regularFamily A) (cellTower A hA c s₀)
    (towerHom A hA c s₀) (fun n => ?_)
    (fun _ m ψ => exists_comp_eq_of_surjective A hA c s₀ hc ⟨m, ψ⟩)
    (fun n _ m ρ h => exists_comp_map_eq_zero A hA c s₀ n m ρ h)
  exact cellTower_map_succ_comp_towerHom A hA c s₀ n

/-- **Stacks 0CRM.** *"Let `(A, d)` be a differential graded algebra with `H^i(A)` countable for
each `i`. Let `M` be an object of `D(A, d)`. Then the following are equivalent: (1)
`M = hocolim Eₙ` with `Eₙ` compact in `D(A, d)`, and (2) `H^i(M)` is countable for each `i`."*

The implication (1) ⇒ (2) is
`DifferentialGradedModule.countable_cohomology_of_isHomotopyColimit_of_isCompactObject` and the
implication (2) ⇒ (1) is
`DifferentialGradedModule.exists_isHomotopyColimit_isCompactObject_of_countable_cohomology`.
Compactness is read in the universe-restricted spelling `IsCompactObject.{u}` on both sides; see
the implementation notes of this file. -/
@[stacks 0CRM]
theorem exists_isHomotopyColimit_isCompactObject_iff_countable_cohomology
    (hA : ∀ k : ℤ, Countable (A.Cohomology k)) (M : derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A) :
    (∃ F : ℕ ⥤ derivedCategory.{u, u, u} A,
        (∀ n : ℕ, IsCompactObject.{u} (F.obj n)) ∧ IsHomotopyColimit F M) ↔
      ∀ i : ℤ, Countable ((cohomologyFunctor A i).obj M) :=
  ⟨fun ⟨F, hF, hFM⟩ i =>
      countable_cohomology_of_isHomotopyColimit_of_isCompactObject A hA M F hF hFM i,
    exists_isHomotopyColimit_isCompactObject_of_countable_cohomology A hA M⟩

end DifferentialGradedModule

